auseKind = \"deadline\"           // ran past its timeout\ninternal/closeout/closeout.go:106:\tCauseRestart   CauseKind = \"controller_restart\" // orphaned, gone or never started across a restart\ninternal/closeout/closeout.go:107:\tCauseNoProcess CauseKind = \"no_process\"         // never started, or its process vanished in this run\ninternal/closeout/closeout.go:108:\tCauseRateLimit CauseKind = \"rate_limit\"         // the agent reported a transient API error\ninternal/closeout/closeout.go:109:\tCauseCloneFail CauseKind = \"clone_failed\"       // a loop's Prepare failed at git or clone\ninternal/closeout/closeout.go:110:\t// CausePushTransient: a push or fetch hit a GitHub 5xx or a network error\ninternal/closeout/closeout.go:112:\tCausePushTransient CauseKind = \"push_transient\"\ninternal/closeout/closeout.go:113:\tCauseEvicted       CauseKind = \"evicted\" // the Kubernetes pod was evicted, deleted or never scheduled\ninternal/closeout/closeout.go:114:\t// CauseInputsTooLarge: the job's untrusted inputs exceed what a pod\ninternal/closeout/closeout.go:116:\tCauseInputsTooLarge CauseKind = \"inputs_too_large\"","numLines":40,"totalLines":77,"appliedLimit":40},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_016Uvo3ahH9YjF6f8itLZmPC","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChPtiAEbgahbfsk2BEbbW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SiR8Y2bVHqH24MopzGY6Fy","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /work; grep -n \"transient\\|Transient\\|stale lease\\|maxPushes\" docs/proposal.md | head -30; grep -n \"Push\\|fake\" internal/committer/transient_test.go | head -30; ls internal/gitx/gittest"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8185,"cache_read_input_tokens":16786,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8185},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"17b7a3de-5f21-40f0-b683-bd56dd09ed54","uuid":"e2416fa0-e495-445b-b2cd-e08169733a2e","timestamp":"2026-11-25T15:35:34.611Z","request_id":"req_011ChPtiA4Sc8gTt2TYs1RvX"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SiR8Y2bVHqH24MopzGY6Fy","type":"tool_result","content":"93:**Retries are explicit.** Every retry, automatic or human, is a new job with its own id (and attempt 1). A human retries a failed job by removing `ard:executor:failed`. The controller retries on its own once, only for a transient cause it can name: a rate limit, a clone failure, a controller restart (the job was orphaned, gone or never started across it), or node eviction under Kubernetes (cause `evicted`, a transient one; `Status.Transient` and `closeout.CauseEvicted` arrive in later parts of #54). The failed job then gets no `ard:executor:failed` and keeps its request label, so the next claim runs it again; that claim is linked to the failed job (`retry_of` in local state), so cost and causes can be traced. An automatic retry that fails again is marked failed. A usage-limit stop is not a failure: it closes out as `usage-limit`, with no `ard:executor:failed` and the request label kept, and it neither uses up nor grants the automatic retry. The re-run after the reset is a new job linked to the stopped one through `after_limit` in local state, which also carries `retry_of` over, so a limit stop of an automatic retry does not earn a second retry. Its comment says the issue is paused for the usage limit until a time. A throttle that the CLI says is \"not your usage limit\" is an ordinary rate-limit retry, not a usage-limit stop. Thresholds and early warnings are [#63](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/63).\n302:**Sidecar repos are context, read-only.** A role's sidecar (for example `vpe-specs`) is cloned by the executor, not the loop: the loop passes the role's resolved sidecar (repo and ref) on the job. The executor keeps one shallow (depth 1) bare object cache per sidecar repo at `~/.ard/sidecars/<controlled-owner>/<controlled-repo>/<sidecar-owner>/<sidecar-repo>.git`, shared by every job of that controlled repo, and exports the commit into the job home as `<job home>/sidecar` (plain files: no `.git`, `chmod -R a-w` in both modes, and a `:ro` mount under Docker). The job sees it at `$ARD_SIDECAR`. `ref` is a branch, tag or full 40-character SHA (an abbreviated SHA is a config error). A SHA already in the cache needs no network; a branch or tag is re-fetched (depth 1) for every job. Whatever the ref, `Prepare` resolves it to a full commit SHA and records it on the job's handle (and in the `executor.sidecar_ready` log), so a job is reproducible. Symlinks and submodules in the sidecar are rejected and the tree is capped at 64 MiB, like config snapshots. A fetch failure (network, auth, a stale lock, which the executor clears by rebuilding the cache once) is the transient `clone_failed`; a ref or SHA that does not exist, or a repo the App cannot read, is a permanent failure that names the sidecar. A role that wants a spec changed says so in `plan.md`; editing the sidecar is out of scope for v1.\n325:**Serve wiring ([#318](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/318)).** `executor:` in `.ard/config.yaml` picks the executor at startup and it is fixed for the life of the process: a later config whose `executor` differs is a config error (serve pauses, the status issue says `restart ard serve`). Cluster settings are `ard serve` flags with `ARD_K8S_*` env fallbacks, set by the chart: `--k8s-namespace`, `--k8s-instance`, `--k8s-agent-image`, `--k8s-pod-image` (both images pinned by `@sha256:` digest), `--k8s-git-base` (default `https://github.com`), `--k8s-resources` (`cpu=`, `memory=`, `memory-limit=`, `ephemeral-storage=` for the agent container), `--k8s-pre-budget` (5m) and `--k8s-result-grace` (2m). With `executor: k8s`, startup fails (exit 1) if the credential is a personal access token (also under `--dry-run`), a required flag is missing, or the config sets `roles_repo` (not supported with k8s yet). If no config has loaded and the cluster flags are set, the mode is unknown and no job loops are built until restart (`serve.executor_unresolved`). At startup the `pool-adopt` hook calls the executor's `Sweep`, which deletes this repo's job pods and Secrets that no running job record owns; pods with a record go through the Reaper's normal `Orphaned` path. The Reaper maps the executor's end reasons to causes: `evicted` (pod evicted, deleted or never scheduled; transient, one automatic retry), `clone_failed` (transient), and `no_process` for a failed prep step, bad result or missing result. Inputs over the manifest cap close out as `inputs_too_large` and are not retried. Live read tokens are added to the redaction set. `ard config check` prints a note that cluster settings come from `ard serve` flags.\n331:**Agent CLIs plug in through adapters.** Each adapter turns (prompt file, context paths, budget) into a command line and parses the exit code and, where the CLI reports them, turns and cost. For Claude Code that is `claude -p --output-format=stream-json --verbose --restricted --safe-mode --strict-mcp-config --disable-slash-commands --permission-mode=dontAsk --settings='{\"includeGitInstructions\":false,\"disableAllHooks\":true}' --tools=… --allowed-tools=… --disallowed-tools=… --model <model> --max-budget-usd <max_budget_usd> --append-system-prompt-file <role prompt> -- <message>` (`--verbose` is required by `stream-json` in print mode). Every stage (scanner, triage, worker, review) gets the same tool profile: `--tools` and `--allowed-tools` are `Read,Glob,Grep,Edit,Write,Bash`, and `--disallowed-tools` is `Bash(git commit *),Bash(git push *),Bash(git remote *),Bash(gh *)` (decided, #79). ard does not pass `--bare`: it limits the CLI to Bash, Read and Edit, and `--tools` cannot widen it (#105). The planted-config isolation `--bare` gave comes instead from `--restricted` (only managed settings and `--settings` load), `--safe-mode` (no CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md, skills, agents, hooks, MCP servers or auto memory), `--strict-mcp-config`, `--disable-slash-commands`, and `--settings` that turns off hooks and the git status snapshot (commit subjects are third-party text). `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_BACKGROUND_TASKS=1` is set as a fixed env var (the executor allows it) so Bash cannot detach commands. The integration tests pin each of these against a worktree with planted config and must be re-run on every `TestedVersion` bump. The tool list is not a security boundary, and the deny list is advisory, because shell indirection gets around it. The real boundaries are the missing GitHub token (Docker; see the host-mode note above, #84), the committer, which commits only `.ard/<issue#>/` from a triage or review job (other edits are discarded with the worktree), and the diff guard on worker changes. An explicit tool list from the caller (`Spec.Tools`) replaces the allow list and keeps the deny list. The user message holds only fixed text and the paths of the untrusted input files. The installed CLI accepts an undocumented `--max-turns`; ard does not rely on it, so a per-job dollar cap and the executor's hard timeout bound each run. Agents run with an empty per-job CLI config dir and HOME and receive exactly one model credential, passed by name by the executor and never visible to the adapter: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) or a subscription OAuth token (`CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`, from `claude setup-token`); that rests on the environment, not on a CLI flag. `ard serve` chooses the credential from its own environment: `ARD_AGENT_AUTH` is `auto` (the default; the token if set, else the key), `oauth` or `apikey`, and an explicit mode never falls back to the other variable. Only one is ever passed, because the CLI prefers the API key and would silently bill it (pinned by an integration test). A missing credential does not stop startup, because deterministic scanners need none: `ard serve` logs one INFO line, and a job that needs one fails with a clear error. The executor creates the per-job config dir as `<job home>/.claude` (0700) in `prepare` (`Handle.View.ConfigDir`; under Docker it is a bind mount at `/ard-config`), refuses to start a job whose config dir is not that empty directory, and removes it with the job home; the Reaper's `KeepWorktree` retention keeps it too, and it holds no credential, since the credential travels only in the environment. `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBPROCESS_ENV_SCRUB=1`, which would keep the token out of Bash children, is not set (decided, #125). Residual risk: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) is inherited by Bash children; on the tested release the CLI does not pass `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` to them; and either credential can still be read from the `claude` process's own environment (`/proc/$PPID/environ` on Linux, `ps eww` on macOS). The container is the boundary: it holds no GitHub token, and the credential is scoped to the model. The scrub is off because on 2.1.328 it requires bubblewrap (and socat) plus unprivileged user namespaces; Docker blocks those under ard's flags (`--cap-drop ALL`, `no-new-privileges`, default seccomp), so every Bash call would fail closed and without bwrap the CLI refuses to start. On macOS host mode it worked and matched the `dontAsk` outcomes. It can be revisited for host mode or a loosened container. What agent Bash can read differs by mode: under Docker, only the job's own credential; in host mode, far more, because any same-uid process's environment is readable (`ps eww <pid>` on macOS, `/proc/<pid>/environ` on Linux), including `ard serve`'s own, which holds both credentials. The scrub would not close that; it is part of the host-mode risk of #84. `kimi` and `dsh-tui` both run headless with `-p`; neither has a turn cap and `kimi` reports no cost, so turns and cost are optional in the adapter result. `config.yaml` names the default CLI and any per-role override, so a role can move between CLIs without touching its prompt. The Claude adapter also reads a usage limit and token usage from the stream: a limit comes from a `rate_limit_event` with status `rejected` (its `resetsAt` is the reset time), from the CLI's own error text (`You've hit your session, weekly, Opus or Sonnet limit`) and from `rate_limit` or `billing_error` errors, and it records `usage` and `modelUsage` token counts (by input, output, cache write and cache read; per model when the result has `modelUsage`, which is preferred for accounting). That text is matched only on paths the CLI itself writes (an assistant message carrying an `error`, or an errored result with HTTP 429 or `api_error`), never in agent output, because an agent can be made to print the phrase. Only the job's own agent step and its fix steps are read for a limit: a worker's test step runs agent-written code, so its output is never classified (a printed limit line there cannot block the account). With an API key a 429 stays a transient `rate_limit` retry; only subscription (OAuth) runs have usage limits. The adapter also reads `rate_limit_info.unifiedWindows` from every `rate_limit_event` into `Result.Windows` (one reading per window type, the last winning, also when the run ends with no result). The controller records the readings for OAuth steps only, from the job's own agent and fix steps and never from a test step, including killed steps with no result; it logs them as `limits.reading`. A recording error is logged as `limits.observe_failed` and never fails the job. Nothing is gated on the readings yet (#63). The committed fixtures are scrubbed real captures where a human has made them (`testdata/CAPTURE.md`) and clearly named `synthetic_*` files elsewhere.\n335:**Close-out is resumable.** The controller (`internal/closeout`) decides the outcome from the role file (only one this job wrote), stages the redacted files, then commits, requests the `next` labels, posts one summary comment (for a review with a verdict, one PR review instead; either is tagged `<!-- ard:closeout job=<id> -->` so a replay never posts twice) and finally swaps `:running` for `:done`, `:changes`, `ard:needs-human` or `ard:executor:failed`. It records its phase in local state after each step. `serve` resumes unfinished close-outs at startup, and the Reaper resumes, rather than fails, a job whose close-out has started. Three more outcomes exist for a job that ended without a usable result: `failed` (sets `ard:executor:failed`), `retry` (a named transient cause: no `ard:executor:failed`, the request label kept, the next claim is the automatic retry) and `usage-limit` (a stop at a subscription usage limit: `:running` is removed, nothing is added, the request label stays, and the issue is claimed again after the reset without using up the retry). All three commit only the redacted log. Their comment carries the reason, the last 50 log lines (cut from the redacted log, scanned again and capped at 8 KiB; if that scan flags or fails, the comment says the tail was withheld) and when the worktree and host files are kept until. `next` entries are applied only for roles that exist; a `review:<role>` the lead requests is applied when the worker opens the PR, not at triage close-out. Only changed files under `.ard/<issue#>/` that the job's allowlist permits are committed, so a newer `issue.md` on `main` is never overwritten.\n342:2. **Close-out.** Commit the partial output to `.ard/<issue#>/logs/` (on `main` or the branch, as a normal close-out would), post one comment with the reason, the last 50 redacted log lines and the time the evidence is kept until, then remove `ard:<stage>:<role>:running` and add `ard:executor:failed`. A transient cause instead hands off to the automatic retry, and a usage limit pauses the issue until the reset (see Retries are explicit). A log that cannot be committed (a worker that stopped before its first push has no branch) never turns a retry into a failure.\n525:- **The GitHub client never follows redirects.** A redirect can turn a write into a silent GET, replay it into another repository, or carry the token to another host. An issue that redirects to another repository (transferred) reads as not found. A repository that redirects (renamed or transferred) is a hard, non-transient error naming the new name when GitHub reveals it. ard does not follow a rename; point it at the new name. A deleted issue (410 Gone) also reads as not found.\n25:func (f *flaky) Push(ctx context.Context, op effects.PushOp) error {\n28:\t\treturn f.Effects.Push(ctx, op)\n32:\t\tif err := f.Effects.Push(ctx, op); err != nil {\n62:func TestTransientPushLanded(t *testing.T) {\ngittest.go","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"17b7a3de-5f21-40f0-b683-bd56dd09ed54","uuid":"87ef8a65-a26e-4787-be94-81a190fa7f39","timestamp":"2026-11-25T15:35:34.641Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"93:**Retries are explicit.** Every retry, automatic or human, is a new job with its own id (and attempt 1). A human retries a failed job by removing `ard:executor:failed`. The controller retries on its own once, only for a transient cause it can name: a rate limit, a clone failure, a controller restart (the job was orphaned, gone or never started across it), or node eviction under Kubernetes (cause `evicted`, a transient one; `Status.Transient` and `closeout.CauseEvicted` arrive in later parts of #54). The failed job then gets no `ard:executor:failed` and keeps its request label, so the next claim runs it again; that claim is linked to the failed job (`retry_of` in local state), so cost and causes can be traced. An automatic retry that fails again is marked failed. A usage-limit stop is not a failure: it closes out as `usage-limit`, with no `ard:executor:failed` and the request label kept, and it neither uses up nor grants the automatic retry. The re-run after the reset is a new job linked to the stopped one through `after_limit` in local state, which also carries `retry_of` over, so a limit stop of an automatic retry does not earn a second retry. Its comment says the issue is paused for the usage limit until a time. A throttle that the CLI says i